1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,600

2
00:00:06,600 --> 00:00:11,230
Hello, it's fun to be here.

3
00:00:11,230 --> 00:00:14,760
So the work I'm presenting
today, the title of it

4
00:00:14,760 --> 00:00:18,060
is Attention Approximates
Sparse Distributed Memory,

5
00:00:18,060 --> 00:00:19,590
and this was done
in collaboration

6
00:00:19,590 --> 00:00:24,540
with Cengiz Pehlevan, and my
PhD advisor was Gabriel Kreiman.

7
00:00:24,540 --> 00:00:27,060
So why should you
care about this work?

8
00:00:27,060 --> 00:00:29,280
We show that the heuristic
attention operation

9
00:00:29,280 --> 00:00:31,260
can be implemented
with simple properties

10
00:00:31,260 --> 00:00:34,630
of high dimensional vectors in a
biologically plausible fashion.

11
00:00:34,630 --> 00:00:37,580
So the transformer and
attention, as you know,

12
00:00:37,580 --> 00:00:40,860
are incredibly powerful,
but they were heuristically

13
00:00:40,860 --> 00:00:43,950
developed, and the softmax
operation in attention

14
00:00:43,950 --> 00:00:47,160
is particularly important
but also heuristic.

15
00:00:47,160 --> 00:00:50,730
And so, we show that the
intersection of hyperspheres

16
00:00:50,730 --> 00:00:53,520
that is used in sparse
distributed memory closely

17
00:00:53,520 --> 00:00:57,370
approximates the softmax
and attention more broadly,

18
00:00:57,370 --> 00:00:59,940
both in theory and with
some experiments on train

19
00:00:59,940 --> 00:01:01,330
transformers.

20
00:01:01,330 --> 00:01:04,379
So you can see SDM,
Sparse Distributed Memory,

21
00:01:04,379 --> 00:01:07,245
as preempting attention
by approximately 30 years.

22
00:01:07,245 --> 00:01:11,290
It was developed back in 1988,
and what's exciting about this

23
00:01:11,290 --> 00:01:13,727
is that it meets a high bar
for biological plausibility.

24
00:01:13,727 --> 00:01:15,310
Hopefully, I have
time to actually get

25
00:01:15,310 --> 00:01:16,727
into the wiring
of the cerebellum,

26
00:01:16,727 --> 00:01:18,790
and how you can
map each operation

27
00:01:18,790 --> 00:01:21,770
to part of the circuit there.

28
00:01:21,770 --> 00:01:24,340
So first, I'm going to give an
overview of Sparse Distributed

29
00:01:24,340 --> 00:01:26,620
Memory.

30
00:01:26,620 --> 00:01:28,840
I have a transformer
attention summary,

31
00:01:28,840 --> 00:01:31,420
but I assume that you guys
already know all of that.

32
00:01:31,420 --> 00:01:35,270
We can get there and then decide
how deep we want to go into it.

33
00:01:35,270 --> 00:01:38,020
I'll then talk about how
actually attention approximates

34
00:01:38,020 --> 00:01:39,850
SDM.

35
00:01:39,850 --> 00:01:43,210
Interpret the transformer more
broadly, and then hopefully,

36
00:01:43,210 --> 00:01:46,780
there's time to go into SDM's
biological plausibility.

37
00:01:46,780 --> 00:01:48,280
Also, I'm going
to keep everything

38
00:01:48,280 --> 00:01:51,700
high level visual intuition,
and then go into the math.

39
00:01:51,700 --> 00:01:55,900
But stop me, and please ask
questions, literally, whenever.

40
00:01:55,900 --> 00:01:56,560
OK.

41
00:01:56,560 --> 00:01:59,470
So Sparse Distributed
Memory is motivated

42
00:01:59,470 --> 00:02:01,630
by the question of how the
brain can read and write

43
00:02:01,630 --> 00:02:05,007
memories in order to later
retrieve the correct one.

44
00:02:05,007 --> 00:02:07,090
And some considerations
that it takes into account

45
00:02:07,090 --> 00:02:10,539
are high memory capacity,
robustness to query noise,

46
00:02:10,539 --> 00:02:14,590
biological plausibility, and
some notion of fault tolerance.

47
00:02:14,590 --> 00:02:17,650
SDM is unique from other
associative memory models

48
00:02:17,650 --> 00:02:20,230
that you may be familiar
with, like hopfield networks.

49
00:02:20,230 --> 00:02:24,130
Insomuch as it's
sparse, so it operates

50
00:02:24,130 --> 00:02:25,840
at a very high
dimensional vector space,

51
00:02:25,840 --> 00:02:28,240
and the neurons that
exist in this space

52
00:02:28,240 --> 00:02:32,560
only occupy a very small
portion of possible locations.

53
00:02:32,560 --> 00:02:35,440
It's also distributed, so
all read and write operations

54
00:02:35,440 --> 00:02:40,040
apply to all nearby neurons.

55
00:02:40,040 --> 00:02:42,710
It is actually, as a side
note, hopfield networks,

56
00:02:42,710 --> 00:02:44,710
if you're familiar with
them, are a special case

57
00:02:44,710 --> 00:02:46,415
of sparse distributed memory.

58
00:02:46,415 --> 00:02:48,040
I'm not going to go
deep into that now,

59
00:02:48,040 --> 00:02:52,070
but I have a blog post on it.

60
00:02:52,070 --> 00:02:52,570
OK.

61
00:02:52,570 --> 00:02:55,900
So first, we're going to look at
the write operation for sparse

62
00:02:55,900 --> 00:02:57,160
distributed memory.

63
00:02:57,160 --> 00:03:00,880
We're in this high dimensional
binary vector space.

64
00:03:00,880 --> 00:03:03,430
We're using hamming distance
as our metric for now,

65
00:03:03,430 --> 00:03:05,530
we'll move to continuous later.

66
00:03:05,530 --> 00:03:10,120
And we have this
green pattern, which

67
00:03:10,120 --> 00:03:12,700
is represented by the solid
dot, and the hollow circles

68
00:03:12,700 --> 00:03:14,890
are these hypothetical neurons.

69
00:03:14,890 --> 00:03:17,350
Also, think of everything
quite abstractly,

70
00:03:17,350 --> 00:03:20,090
and then we'll map
to biology later.

71
00:03:20,090 --> 00:03:22,310
So this pattern
has a write radius,

72
00:03:22,310 --> 00:03:24,040
which is some hamming distance.

73
00:03:24,040 --> 00:03:27,760
It activates all of the neurons
within that hamming distance,

74
00:03:27,760 --> 00:03:31,780
and then here I just denote
that each of those neurons

75
00:03:31,780 --> 00:03:34,240
are now storing
that green pattern,

76
00:03:34,240 --> 00:03:36,130
and the green pattern
has disappeared.

77
00:03:36,130 --> 00:03:37,630
So I'm keeping track
of its location

78
00:03:37,630 --> 00:03:39,213
with this kind of
fuzzy hollow circle.

79
00:03:39,213 --> 00:03:40,690
That will be relevant later.

80
00:03:40,690 --> 00:03:44,380
So we're writing in another
pattern, this orange one,

81
00:03:44,380 --> 00:03:47,920
and note here that neurons can
store multiple patterns inside

82
00:03:47,920 --> 00:03:48,820
of them.

83
00:03:48,820 --> 00:03:51,100
And formally, this is actually
a superposition or just

84
00:03:51,100 --> 00:03:53,110
a summation of these
high dimensional vectors.

85
00:03:53,110 --> 00:03:54,100
Because they're
high dimensional,

86
00:03:54,100 --> 00:03:55,180
you don't have that
much crosstalk,

87
00:03:55,180 --> 00:03:56,330
so you can get away with it.

88
00:03:56,330 --> 00:03:58,080
But for now, you can
even just think of it

89
00:03:58,080 --> 00:04:02,360
as a neuron can store
multiple patterns.

90
00:04:02,360 --> 00:04:04,630
Finally, we have a third
pattern, this blue one.

91
00:04:04,630 --> 00:04:06,880
We're writing in
another location.

92
00:04:06,880 --> 00:04:10,013
And yeah, so again,
we're keeping

93
00:04:10,013 --> 00:04:11,680
track of the original
pattern locations,

94
00:04:11,680 --> 00:04:13,055
and they can be--
but they can be

95
00:04:13,055 --> 00:04:17,360
triangulated from the nearby
neurons that are storing them.

96
00:04:17,360 --> 00:04:19,220
And so we've written
in three patterns.

97
00:04:19,220 --> 00:04:20,720
Now we want to read
from the system.

98
00:04:20,720 --> 00:04:23,470
So I have this pink stars side.

99
00:04:23,470 --> 00:04:26,620
It appears-- it has a
given It's represented

100
00:04:26,620 --> 00:04:29,380
by a given vector, which
has a location in the space.

101
00:04:29,380 --> 00:04:31,600
It activates nearby
neurons again,

102
00:04:31,600 --> 00:04:35,710
but now the neurons
output the patterns

103
00:04:35,710 --> 00:04:37,130
that they've stored previously.

104
00:04:37,130 --> 00:04:40,910
And so you can see that
based upon its location,

105
00:04:40,910 --> 00:04:45,370
it's getting four blue patterns,
two orange, and one green.

106
00:04:45,370 --> 00:04:47,620
And it then does a
majority rule operation,

107
00:04:47,620 --> 00:04:50,170
where it updates
towards whatever pattern

108
00:04:50,170 --> 00:04:51,140
it sees the most.

109
00:04:51,140 --> 00:04:54,790
So in this case because
blue is actually a majority,

110
00:04:54,790 --> 00:04:57,340
it's just going to update
completely towards blue.

111
00:04:57,340 --> 00:04:59,390
Again, I'll formalize
this more in a bit,

112
00:04:59,390 --> 00:05:01,360
but this is really
to give you intuition

113
00:05:01,360 --> 00:05:05,360
for the core operations of SDM.

114
00:05:05,360 --> 00:05:10,000
So the key thing to relate
this back to attention

115
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:12,730
is actually to abstract
away the neurons that

116
00:05:12,730 --> 00:05:15,340
are operating under the hood,
and just consider these circle

117
00:05:15,340 --> 00:05:16,240
intersections.

118
00:05:16,240 --> 00:05:18,850
And so what each of
these intersections

119
00:05:18,850 --> 00:05:23,260
between the pink circle and
each of the write circles means

120
00:05:23,260 --> 00:05:26,020
is the intersection
is the neurons that

121
00:05:26,020 --> 00:05:28,660
both store that pattern
when it was written in,

122
00:05:28,660 --> 00:05:31,570
and are now being read
from by the query.

123
00:05:31,570 --> 00:05:34,570
And the size of
that intersection

124
00:05:34,570 --> 00:05:37,420
corresponds to how
many patterns the query

125
00:05:37,420 --> 00:05:38,500
is then going to read.

126
00:05:38,500 --> 00:05:43,090

127
00:05:43,090 --> 00:05:45,700
And so formally, we
define the number

128
00:05:45,700 --> 00:05:47,950
of neurons in this
circle intersection

129
00:05:47,950 --> 00:05:52,000
as the cardinality between
number of neurons in pattern,

130
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:54,981
number of neurons in query
and their intersection.

131
00:05:54,981 --> 00:05:58,670

132
00:05:58,670 --> 00:05:59,240
OK.

133
00:05:59,240 --> 00:06:01,280
Are there any questions
at a high level

134
00:06:01,280 --> 00:06:05,300
before I get more into the math?

135
00:06:05,300 --> 00:06:06,580
I don't know if I can check.

136
00:06:06,580 --> 00:06:08,340
Is it easy for me to check Zoom?

137
00:06:08,340 --> 00:06:10,355
Nah, sorry, Zoom people,
I'm not going to check.

138
00:06:10,355 --> 00:06:10,855
OK.

139
00:06:10,855 --> 00:06:13,555
Are the neurons just randomly
distributed in the space?

140
00:06:13,555 --> 00:06:14,055
Yes.

141
00:06:14,055 --> 00:06:15,350
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

142
00:06:15,350 --> 00:06:19,507
And there's later-- there's more
recent work that they can learn

143
00:06:19,507 --> 00:06:21,590
and update their location
to [INAUDIBLE] manifold,

144
00:06:21,590 --> 00:06:24,230
but in this, you can assume
that they're randomly

145
00:06:24,230 --> 00:06:27,440
initialized binary high
dimensional vectors.

146
00:06:27,440 --> 00:06:28,010
OK.

147
00:06:28,010 --> 00:06:34,160
So this is the full
SDM update rule.

148
00:06:34,160 --> 00:06:35,910
I'm going to break it down.

149
00:06:35,910 --> 00:06:38,450
So the first thing that you do.

150
00:06:38,450 --> 00:06:40,280
So this is for
reading, to be clear.

151
00:06:40,280 --> 00:06:42,690
So you've already written
patterns into your neurons.

152
00:06:42,690 --> 00:06:46,070
So the first thing you do
is you weight each pattern

153
00:06:46,070 --> 00:06:47,970
by the size of its
circle intersection.

154
00:06:47,970 --> 00:06:53,280
So the circle intersection
there for each pattern.

155
00:06:53,280 --> 00:06:57,240
Then you sum over all of
the patterns that have

156
00:06:57,240 --> 00:06:58,580
been written into the space.

157
00:06:58,580 --> 00:07:01,500
So you're just doing a
weighted summation of them.

158
00:07:01,500 --> 00:07:03,900
And then there's
this normalization

159
00:07:03,900 --> 00:07:09,390
by the total number of
intersections that you have.

160
00:07:09,390 --> 00:07:11,240
And finally, because
at least for now we're

161
00:07:11,240 --> 00:07:14,840
working in this binary space,
you map back to binary,

162
00:07:14,840 --> 00:07:18,200
just seeing if the values
are greater than one half.

163
00:07:18,200 --> 00:07:21,040

164
00:07:21,040 --> 00:07:22,300
OK.

165
00:07:22,300 --> 00:07:24,272
How familiar are
people with attention?

166
00:07:24,272 --> 00:07:26,230
I looked at like the
previous talks you've had,

167
00:07:26,230 --> 00:07:27,940
they seem quite high level.

168
00:07:27,940 --> 00:07:31,360
Like, can you guys write the
attention equation for me?

169
00:07:31,360 --> 00:07:36,900
Is that like-- can I get
thumbs up if you can do that?

170
00:07:36,900 --> 00:07:37,920
Yeah.

171
00:07:37,920 --> 00:07:39,840
OK, I'm not like--

172
00:07:39,840 --> 00:07:42,300
I'll go through this, but
I'll probably go through it

173
00:07:42,300 --> 00:07:43,900
faster than otherwise.

174
00:07:43,900 --> 00:07:45,960
So when I first made
this presentation, like,

175
00:07:45,960 --> 00:07:48,180
this was the state of the
art for transformers, which

176
00:07:48,180 --> 00:07:51,750
was like [INAUDIBLE] alphafold.

177
00:07:51,750 --> 00:07:54,390
And so it's kind of funny
how far things have come now.

178
00:07:54,390 --> 00:07:57,960
I don't need to tell you that
transformers are important.

179
00:07:57,960 --> 00:08:00,380
So yeah.

180
00:08:00,380 --> 00:08:02,070
I'm going to work
with this example.

181
00:08:02,070 --> 00:08:04,860
Well, OK, I'm going to work
with this example here,

182
00:08:04,860 --> 00:08:07,020
the cat sat on the blank.

183
00:08:07,020 --> 00:08:10,160
And so in this setting,
we're predicting

184
00:08:10,160 --> 00:08:12,710
the next token,
which hypothetically

185
00:08:12,710 --> 00:08:14,450
is the word "mat."

186
00:08:14,450 --> 00:08:17,810
And so there are four things
that the attention operation

187
00:08:17,810 --> 00:08:18,830
is doing.

188
00:08:18,830 --> 00:08:20,630
The first one up here
is it's generating

189
00:08:20,630 --> 00:08:22,297
what are called keys
values and queries.

190
00:08:22,297 --> 00:08:24,213
And again, I'll get into
the math in a second,

191
00:08:24,213 --> 00:08:26,420
I'm just trying to keep
it high level first.

192
00:08:26,420 --> 00:08:30,110
And then we're going
to compare our query

193
00:08:30,110 --> 00:08:31,860
with each of the keys.

194
00:08:31,860 --> 00:08:34,340
So the word "the," which is
closest to the word we're next

195
00:08:34,340 --> 00:08:37,159
predicting is our
query, and we're

196
00:08:37,159 --> 00:08:42,980
seeing how similar is
each of the key vectors.

197
00:08:42,980 --> 00:08:45,620
We then, based upon
that similarity,

198
00:08:45,620 --> 00:08:50,780
do this softmax normalization
so that all of the attention

199
00:08:50,780 --> 00:08:52,910
weights sum to 1.

200
00:08:52,910 --> 00:08:55,190
And then we sum
together their value

201
00:08:55,190 --> 00:08:58,730
vectors to use to
propagate to the next layer

202
00:08:58,730 --> 00:09:01,920
or uses our prediction.

203
00:09:01,920 --> 00:09:04,400
And so at a high
level, you can think

204
00:09:04,400 --> 00:09:06,620
of this as, like,
the query word "the"

205
00:09:06,620 --> 00:09:10,070
is looking for nouns and
their associated verbs.

206
00:09:10,070 --> 00:09:12,890
And so hypothetically,
it has a high similarity

207
00:09:12,890 --> 00:09:17,070
with words like cat and
sat or they're keys.

208
00:09:17,070 --> 00:09:19,010
So this then gives
large weight to the cat

209
00:09:19,010 --> 00:09:22,220
and sat value vectors,
which get moved

210
00:09:22,220 --> 00:09:23,810
to the next part of the network.

211
00:09:23,810 --> 00:09:27,020
And the cat value
vector, hypothetically,

212
00:09:27,020 --> 00:09:28,860
contains a superposition
of other animals,

213
00:09:28,860 --> 00:09:33,110
like mice and maybe words
that rhyme with mat.

214
00:09:33,110 --> 00:09:35,090
And the sat vector
also contains things

215
00:09:35,090 --> 00:09:36,830
that are sat on, including mat.

216
00:09:36,830 --> 00:09:40,100
And so what you actually
get from the value

217
00:09:40,100 --> 00:09:45,260
vectors of paying attention to
cat and sat are like, 3 times

218
00:09:45,260 --> 00:09:48,883
mat plus 1 times mouse
plus 1 times sofa.

219
00:09:48,883 --> 00:09:51,050
This is again, like, a
totally hypothetical example,

220
00:09:51,050 --> 00:09:55,700
but I'm trying to make the
point that you can extract

221
00:09:55,700 --> 00:09:59,240
from your value vectors
things useful for predicting

222
00:09:59,240 --> 00:10:02,750
the next token by paying
attention to specific keys.

223
00:10:02,750 --> 00:10:06,960

224
00:10:06,960 --> 00:10:08,022
So-- and I guess--

225
00:10:08,022 --> 00:10:10,480
yeah, another thing here is
like what you pay attention to.

226
00:10:10,480 --> 00:10:12,390
So cat and stat might
be different from what

227
00:10:12,390 --> 00:10:13,590
you're actually extracting.

228
00:10:13,590 --> 00:10:15,257
If you're paying
attention to your keys,

229
00:10:15,257 --> 00:10:17,370
but you're getting
your value vectors out.

230
00:10:17,370 --> 00:10:20,340
OK, so here is the full
attention equation.

231
00:10:20,340 --> 00:10:24,450
The top line, I'm separating
out the projection matrices,

232
00:10:24,450 --> 00:10:27,150
W subscript V [INAUDIBLE] and
q, and then the second one,

233
00:10:27,150 --> 00:10:30,600
I just collapsed them into the
equation you are going to see.

234
00:10:30,600 --> 00:10:34,530
And yeah, so
breaking this apart,

235
00:10:34,530 --> 00:10:37,620
the first step here
is we compare--

236
00:10:37,620 --> 00:10:41,610
we do a dot product between
our query vector and our keys.

237
00:10:41,610 --> 00:10:43,260
This should actually
be a small q

238
00:10:43,260 --> 00:10:45,520
and capital Q. Sorry for that.

239
00:10:45,520 --> 00:10:48,810
And so yeah, we're doing
this dot product between them

240
00:10:48,810 --> 00:10:49,680
to see--

241
00:10:49,680 --> 00:10:52,470
get a notion of similarity.

242
00:10:52,470 --> 00:10:54,840
We then apply the
softmax operation,

243
00:10:54,840 --> 00:10:58,230
which is an exponential
over a sum of exponentials.

244
00:10:58,230 --> 00:11:00,150
The way to think of
the softmax is it just

245
00:11:00,150 --> 00:11:03,270
makes large values larger.

246
00:11:03,270 --> 00:11:05,920
And this will be
important for the relation

247
00:11:05,920 --> 00:11:09,310
to SDM, so I'll
spend a minute on it.

248
00:11:09,310 --> 00:11:12,310
At the top here, I have
some hypothetical items--

249
00:11:12,310 --> 00:11:14,710
index from 0 to 9.

250
00:11:14,710 --> 00:11:17,530
And then the values for
each of those items.

251
00:11:17,530 --> 00:11:20,620
In the second row, I just do
like a normal normalization

252
00:11:20,620 --> 00:11:21,170
of them.

253
00:11:21,170 --> 00:11:25,310
And so the top item
