504
00:23:17,680 --> 00:23:18,970
fall within the bounds.

505
00:23:18,970 --> 00:23:24,003
And two, they skew towards
the max query noise, which

506
00:23:24,003 --> 00:23:25,420
makes more sense
if you're dealing

507
00:23:25,420 --> 00:23:28,528
with complicated real world
data where the next data points

508
00:23:28,528 --> 00:23:30,070
you see might be
out of distribution,

509
00:23:30,070 --> 00:23:32,140
based on what you've
seen in the past.

510
00:23:32,140 --> 00:23:35,930
The maxmemory capacity variant
assumes no query noise at all.

511
00:23:35,930 --> 00:23:38,110
And so it's like, how
many things can I pack in,

512
00:23:38,110 --> 00:23:41,080
assuming that the questions
I'm asking the system

513
00:23:41,080 --> 00:23:42,580
are perfectly formed?

514
00:23:42,580 --> 00:23:46,010

515
00:23:46,010 --> 00:23:50,330
OK, just talking a little bit
about transformer components

516
00:23:50,330 --> 00:23:52,610
more broadly.

517
00:23:52,610 --> 00:23:55,940
So I've mentioned that you can
write the feedforward layer

518
00:23:55,940 --> 00:24:00,500
as a version of SDM, that has
a sort of notion of longer term

519
00:24:00,500 --> 00:24:03,230
memory.

520
00:24:03,230 --> 00:24:06,410
There's also LayerNorm, which
is crucial in transformers.

521
00:24:06,410 --> 00:24:08,270
And it's not quite
the same, but it

522
00:24:08,270 --> 00:24:10,610
can be related to the
L2 normalization that's

523
00:24:10,610 --> 00:24:12,560
required by SDM.

524
00:24:12,560 --> 00:24:15,080
There's also the key query
normalization variant

525
00:24:15,080 --> 00:24:17,060
that explicitly does
this L2 normalization.

526
00:24:17,060 --> 00:24:19,400
And it does get slightly
better performance, at least

527
00:24:19,400 --> 00:24:21,830
on the small tests
that they did.

528
00:24:21,830 --> 00:24:26,300
I don't know if this would
scale to larger models.

529
00:24:26,300 --> 00:24:30,045
And so, I guess this work
is interesting in so much as

530
00:24:30,045 --> 00:24:31,670
like the biological
plausibility, which

531
00:24:31,670 --> 00:24:32,780
I'm about to get to.

532
00:24:32,780 --> 00:24:35,450
And then, the links
to transformers.

533
00:24:35,450 --> 00:24:38,542
It hasn't, to date, improved
transformer architectures.

534
00:24:38,542 --> 00:24:41,000
But that doesn't mean that this
lens couldn't be used or be

535
00:24:41,000 --> 00:24:43,190
useful in some way.

536
00:24:43,190 --> 00:24:45,980
So yeah, I list a
few other things

537
00:24:45,980 --> 00:24:48,740
that SDM is related to that
could be used to funnel in.

538
00:24:48,740 --> 00:24:52,160
And actually, in the new work
where SDM is continual learner,

539
00:24:52,160 --> 00:24:54,228
we kind of expand the
cerebellar circuit,

540
00:24:54,228 --> 00:24:56,270
look at components of it,
particularly inhibitory

541
00:24:56,270 --> 00:24:57,560
interneurons.

542
00:24:57,560 --> 00:24:59,720
Implement those in a
deep learning model,

543
00:24:59,720 --> 00:25:02,100
and it then becomes much
better at continual learning.

544
00:25:02,100 --> 00:25:05,780
So that was kind of a fun way of
actually using this link to get

545
00:25:05,780 --> 00:25:09,290
better bottom line performance.

546
00:25:09,290 --> 00:25:13,460
OK, so a summary of this
section is basically

547
00:25:13,460 --> 00:25:16,370
just the intersection
between two hyperspheres

548
00:25:16,370 --> 00:25:19,370
approximates an exponential.

549
00:25:19,370 --> 00:25:21,740
And this allows SDM's
read and write operations

550
00:25:21,740 --> 00:25:24,470
to approximate
attention, both in theory

551
00:25:24,470 --> 00:25:26,960
and our limited tests.

552
00:25:26,960 --> 00:25:29,900
And so kind of like big
picture research questions

553
00:25:29,900 --> 00:25:32,690
that could come out of this is,
first, is the transformer so

554
00:25:32,690 --> 00:25:35,900
successful because it's
performing some key cognitive

555
00:25:35,900 --> 00:25:37,220
operation?

556
00:25:37,220 --> 00:25:39,950
The cerebellum is a
very old brain region

557
00:25:39,950 --> 00:25:44,270
used by most organisms,
including fruit flies, maybe

558
00:25:44,270 --> 00:25:47,700
even cephalopods, through like
divergent, but now convergent

559
00:25:47,700 --> 00:25:49,530
evolution.

560
00:25:49,530 --> 00:25:52,350
And then, given that
the transformer has

561
00:25:52,350 --> 00:25:55,770
been so successful
empirically, is SDM actually

562
00:25:55,770 --> 00:25:58,680
the correct theory for
cerebellar function?

563
00:25:58,680 --> 00:26:02,392
And that's still
an open question.

564
00:26:02,392 --> 00:26:04,350
As we learn more and more
about the cerebellum,

565
00:26:04,350 --> 00:26:07,650
there's nothing that yet
disproves SDM as working there.

566
00:26:07,650 --> 00:26:08,442
And I think it's--

567
00:26:08,442 --> 00:26:09,900
I'll go out on a
limb, and say it's

568
00:26:09,900 --> 00:26:12,525
like one of the more compelling
theories for how the cerebellum

569
00:26:12,525 --> 00:26:13,950
is actually working.

570
00:26:13,950 --> 00:26:16,918
Yeah, and so I think this
work kind of motivates looking

571
00:26:16,918 --> 00:26:18,960
more of these questions--
both of these questions

572
00:26:18,960 --> 00:26:19,740
more seriously.

573
00:26:19,740 --> 00:26:22,860

574
00:26:22,860 --> 00:26:24,340
Do we have time?

575
00:26:24,340 --> 00:26:25,890
Cool.

576
00:26:25,890 --> 00:26:30,900
So here's the circuit
that implements SDM.

577
00:26:30,900 --> 00:26:35,490
At the bottom, we have patterns
coming in for either reading

578
00:26:35,490 --> 00:26:36,450
or writing.

579
00:26:36,450 --> 00:26:39,780
And they're going to actually
break down into these slides.

580
00:26:39,780 --> 00:26:42,630
OK, yeah, so first, we
have patterns that come in.

581
00:26:42,630 --> 00:26:44,910
And every neuron
here-- these are

582
00:26:44,910 --> 00:26:46,720
the dendrites of each neuron.

583
00:26:46,720 --> 00:26:48,240
And they're deciding
whether or not

584
00:26:48,240 --> 00:26:50,640
they're going to fire for
the input that comes in.

585
00:26:50,640 --> 00:26:54,040

586
00:26:54,040 --> 00:26:57,550
Then, if the neuron
does fire, and you're

587
00:26:57,550 --> 00:27:00,788
writing in that pattern,
then you simultaneously--

588
00:27:00,788 --> 00:27:02,330
and I'm going to
explain this to you,

589
00:27:02,330 --> 00:27:03,100
you're going to be
like this is crazy,

590
00:27:03,100 --> 00:27:03,790
the brain doesn't do this.

591
00:27:03,790 --> 00:27:05,873
And then, I'm going to
hopefully show you it does.

592
00:27:05,873 --> 00:27:08,200
You not only need to have
the thing the pattern

593
00:27:08,200 --> 00:27:11,350
that activates
neurons, but you need

594
00:27:11,350 --> 00:27:15,490
to have a separate line that
tells the neuron what to store.

595
00:27:15,490 --> 00:27:17,650
And just like you have this
difference between keys

596
00:27:17,650 --> 00:27:20,440
and values, where they can be
different vectors representing

597
00:27:20,440 --> 00:27:22,240
different things,
here, you can have

598
00:27:22,240 --> 00:27:26,050
a key that comes in and tells
the neuron when to activate.

599
00:27:26,050 --> 00:27:28,780
And the value for what
it should actually

600
00:27:28,780 --> 00:27:30,400
store, and [INAUDIBLE].

601
00:27:30,400 --> 00:27:33,520
This is called hetero
associative mapping.

602
00:27:33,520 --> 00:27:37,610
And then, once you're
reading from the system,

603
00:27:37,610 --> 00:27:41,650
you also have your query come
in here, activate neurons,

604
00:27:41,650 --> 00:27:45,610
and those neurons then
output whatever they stored.

605
00:27:45,610 --> 00:27:49,240
And the neurons vector
is this particular column

606
00:27:49,240 --> 00:27:50,380
that it's stored.

607
00:27:50,380 --> 00:27:52,840
And it's, as a
reminder, it's storing

608
00:27:52,840 --> 00:27:54,940
patterns in superposition.

609
00:27:54,940 --> 00:27:59,320
And then, it will dump whatever
it stored across these output

610
00:27:59,320 --> 00:28:00,400
lines.

611
00:28:00,400 --> 00:28:04,930
And then, you have this
G majority bit operation

612
00:28:04,930 --> 00:28:07,900
to convert to a 0 or 1, decide
if the neuron is going to fire

613
00:28:07,900 --> 00:28:09,890
or not.

614
00:28:09,890 --> 00:28:14,190
And so, here is
the same circuit,

615
00:28:14,190 --> 00:28:19,840
but where I overlay cell
types in the cerebellum.

616
00:28:19,840 --> 00:28:22,760
And so I'll come
back to this slide,

617
00:28:22,760 --> 00:28:25,260
because most people probably
aren't familiar with cerebellar

618
00:28:25,260 --> 00:28:27,000
circuitry.

619
00:28:27,000 --> 00:28:31,680
Let me just-- it's in water.

620
00:28:31,680 --> 00:28:34,650
OK, so the way
that the cerebellum

621
00:28:34,650 --> 00:28:37,350
is pretty homogeneous,
in that it follows

622
00:28:37,350 --> 00:28:39,450
this pattern throughout.

623
00:28:39,450 --> 00:28:41,670
Also fun fact, 70% of
all neurons in the brain

624
00:28:41,670 --> 00:28:43,015
are in the cerebellum.

625
00:28:43,015 --> 00:28:44,640
They're small, so
you wouldn't know it,

626
00:28:44,640 --> 00:28:46,650
but the cerebellum is
very underappreciated,

627
00:28:46,650 --> 00:28:48,275
and there's a bunch
of evidence that it

628
00:28:48,275 --> 00:28:51,180
has closed loop systems with
most higher order cognitive

629
00:28:51,180 --> 00:28:52,260
processing now.

630
00:28:52,260 --> 00:28:53,850
If your cerebellum
is damaged, you

631
00:28:53,850 --> 00:28:56,140
are more likely to have
autism, et cetera, et cetera.

632
00:28:56,140 --> 00:28:58,710
So it does a lot more than
just fine motor coordination.

633
00:28:58,710 --> 00:29:01,440
Which a lot of people
have assumed in the past.

634
00:29:01,440 --> 00:29:04,500
OK, so inputs come in through
the muscle fibers here.

635
00:29:04,500 --> 00:29:06,210
They interface
with granule cells.

636
00:29:06,210 --> 00:29:08,490
This is a major up
projection, where you have

637
00:29:08,490 --> 00:29:10,980
tons and tons of granule cells.

638
00:29:10,980 --> 00:29:13,770
Each granule cell has what are
called parallel fibers, which

639
00:29:13,770 --> 00:29:17,130
are incredibly long
and thin axons that

640
00:29:17,130 --> 00:29:19,440
branch out in this T-structure.

641
00:29:19,440 --> 00:29:24,510
And then, they're hit
by the purkinje cells,

642
00:29:24,510 --> 00:29:29,590
which will receive up to
100,000 parallel fiber inputs.

643
00:29:29,590 --> 00:29:33,130
It's the highest connectivity
of any neuron in the brain.

644
00:29:33,130 --> 00:29:36,880
And then, the purkinje cell will
decide whether or not to fire,

645
00:29:36,880 --> 00:29:40,030
and send its output
downwards here.

646
00:29:40,030 --> 00:29:42,498
So that's the whole system
where patterns come in

647
00:29:42,498 --> 00:29:44,290
and the neurons decide
if they fire or not.

648
00:29:44,290 --> 00:29:47,430
And the way that they
then output their output.

649
00:29:47,430 --> 00:29:49,150
You then have a
separate right line,

650
00:29:49,150 --> 00:29:50,890
which is the climbing biopsy.

651
00:29:50,890 --> 00:29:52,757
So the climbing fibers
come up, and they're

652
00:29:52,757 --> 00:29:54,840
pretty amazing in that
these connections here, you

653
00:29:54,840 --> 00:29:56,280
kind of know are
not as important.

654
00:29:56,280 --> 00:29:58,720
The one that really matters is
that they're not very strong,

655
00:29:58,720 --> 00:29:59,010
either.

656
00:29:59,010 --> 00:30:00,510
Or what really
matters is it goes up

657
00:30:00,510 --> 00:30:03,510
and it wraps around
individual purkinje cells.

658
00:30:03,510 --> 00:30:06,240
And the mapping is
close to one to one,

659
00:30:06,240 --> 00:30:08,280
between climbing fibers
and purkinje cells.

660
00:30:08,280 --> 00:30:11,610
And leads to very strong
action potentials.

661
00:30:11,610 --> 00:30:13,320
They're connecting to us here?

662
00:30:13,320 --> 00:30:15,000
In these--

663
00:30:15,000 --> 00:30:16,710
Yes, in the stuff
off to the side?

664
00:30:16,710 --> 00:30:18,810
Yeah, right, these two lines.

665
00:30:18,810 --> 00:30:19,860
They're [INAUDIBLE].

666
00:30:19,860 --> 00:30:23,730
Oh, so they're separate neurons
coming from separate areas.

667
00:30:23,730 --> 00:30:26,197
Purkinje cells go into
deep cerebellar nuclei,

668
00:30:26,197 --> 00:30:27,780
kind of in the core
of the cerebellum.

669
00:30:27,780 --> 00:30:31,057
And that then feeds
into thalamus,

670
00:30:31,057 --> 00:30:32,640
like back to high
order brain regions,

671
00:30:32,640 --> 00:30:36,113
or like down the muscle
movement, et cetera.

672
00:30:36,113 --> 00:30:38,280
A lot of people will think
of the cerebellum as kind

673
00:30:38,280 --> 00:30:41,950
of like a fine-tuning lookup
table, where you've already

674
00:30:41,950 --> 00:30:43,840
decided the muscle
movement you want to do,

675
00:30:43,840 --> 00:30:47,060
but the cerebellum will then
do a bunch of adjustments.

676
00:30:47,060 --> 00:30:49,450
So that it's much more accurate.

677
00:30:49,450 --> 00:30:50,950
But it seems like
this also applies

678
00:30:50,950 --> 00:30:53,050
to next word prediction.

679
00:30:53,050 --> 00:30:54,730
Like we have fMRI data for this.

680
00:30:54,730 --> 00:30:57,250
A neuroscientist once said to
me that a dirty little secret

681
00:30:57,250 --> 00:31:01,810
of fMRI is that the cerebellum
lights up for everything.

682
00:31:01,810 --> 00:31:08,710
So OK, going back to
this circuit here, then.

683
00:31:08,710 --> 00:31:09,210
Yeah--

684
00:31:09,210 --> 00:31:11,610
Timescales, are these
operating again?

685
00:31:11,610 --> 00:31:13,530
I mean, how long
is the information

686
00:31:13,530 --> 00:31:15,390
stored and retrieved?

687
00:31:15,390 --> 00:31:18,480
Do we have any idea about this?

688
00:31:18,480 --> 00:31:20,940
Like it's just like a
couple of milliseconds,

689
00:31:20,940 --> 00:31:23,410
or is this information
more persistent?

690
00:31:23,410 --> 00:31:27,390
So the main theory
is that you have

691
00:31:27,390 --> 00:31:34,050
updating through time-dependent
plasticity, where your climbing

692
00:31:34,050 --> 00:31:37,200
fiber will either-- which is
doing what you want right then,

693
00:31:37,200 --> 00:31:41,880
will fire either just before or
just after your granule cells

694
00:31:41,880 --> 00:31:43,020
fire.

695
00:31:43,020 --> 00:31:45,930
And so that, then,
updates the purkinje cell

696
00:31:45,930 --> 00:31:49,200
synapses for long term
depression or potentiation.

697
00:31:49,200 --> 00:31:51,270
So whatever timescale
that's happening on.

698
00:31:51,270 --> 00:31:54,690
The climbing fiber makes very
large vector potentials--

699
00:31:54,690 --> 00:31:57,550
or it leads to very large vector
potentials within the cell.

700
00:31:57,550 --> 00:32:01,020
And so I do think you could get
pretty fast synaptic updates.

701
00:32:01,020 --> 00:32:04,350
And they're also
persistent for a long time?

702
00:32:04,350 --> 00:32:05,368
I think so, yeah.

703
00:32:05,368 --> 00:32:07,660
The synapses are steady for
like the rest of your life?

704
00:32:07,660 --> 00:32:10,240
Yeah, yeah.

705
00:32:10,240 --> 00:32:13,120
So what's really unique
about this circuit

706
00:32:13,120 --> 00:32:18,062
is the fact that you have these
two orthogonal inputs, where

707
00:32:18,062 --> 00:32:20,020
you have the mossy fibers
bringing information.

708
00:32:20,020 --> 00:32:21,670
And then it's decided if the
neurons are going to fire

709
00:32:21,670 --> 00:32:22,170
or not.

710
00:32:22,170 --> 00:32:24,940
But then the totally
separate fiber lines

711
00:32:24,940 --> 00:32:27,700
that can update
specific neurons,

712
00:32:27,700 --> 00:32:30,310
and what they're storing
and will later output.

713
00:32:30,310 --> 00:32:32,920
And then, the purkinje
cell is so important,

714
00:32:32,920 --> 00:32:37,150
it's kind of doing this pooling
across every single neuron.

715
00:32:37,150 --> 00:32:40,010
And each neuron, remember,
it's storing a vector this way.

716
00:32:40,010 --> 00:32:43,960
And so the purkinje cell is
doing element wise, summation,

717
00:32:43,960 --> 00:32:46,640
and then deciding
whether it fires or not.

718
00:32:46,640 --> 00:32:49,540
And this allows for you to store
your vectors in superposition,

719
00:32:49,540 --> 00:32:51,130
and then later denoise them.

720
00:32:51,130 --> 00:32:56,437

721
00:32:56,437 --> 00:32:58,020
The theory [INAUDIBLE]
maps quite well

722
00:32:58,020 --> 00:33:01,530
to the Marr and Albus theories
of cerebellar function, which

723
00:33:01,530 --> 00:33:03,795
are still quite dominant,
if anyone's familiar

724
00:33:03,795 --> 00:33:05,230
and wants to dive
deep into those.

725
00:33:05,230 --> 00:33:05,730
Yeah?

726
00:33:05,730 --> 00:33:09,180
So the analogy of the neuron
in SDM you introduced before,

727
00:33:09,180 --> 00:33:11,490
was that basically each
neuron in purkinje cell?

728
00:33:11,490 --> 00:33:12,370
In this setting?

729
00:33:12,370 --> 00:33:13,620
Each neuron is a granule cell.

730
00:33:13,620 --> 00:33:16,170

731
00:33:16,170 --> 00:33:19,680
And then, yeah, so the
location of the neuron,

732
00:33:19,680 --> 00:33:22,230
those hollow circles,
corresponds to the granule cell

733
00:33:22,230 --> 00:33:25,385
dendrites here, where
the patterns that pop in

734
00:33:25,385 --> 00:33:27,510
correspond to the activations
of the muscle fibers.

735
00:33:27,510 --> 00:33:33,000
And then, the afferent
postsynaptic connections

736
00:33:33,000 --> 00:33:34,600
are with the purkinje cell.

737
00:33:34,600 --> 00:33:37,830
So that's actually
what it's storing

738
00:33:37,830 --> 00:33:40,140
is in the synaptic
connections with the--

739
00:33:40,140 --> 00:33:43,675
and purkinje cells
at that interface.

740
00:33:43,675 --> 00:33:45,550
And then, the purkinje
cell does the majority

741
00:33:45,550 --> 00:33:47,883
of the operation in deciding
if it wants to fire or not.

742
00:33:47,883 --> 00:33:57,080

743
00:33:57,080 --> 00:33:58,038
Got about five minutes.

744
00:33:58,038 --> 00:33:59,080
Can we ask you questions?

745
00:33:59,080 --> 00:34:01,700
Yeah, yeah, I think we're
basically into question time.

746
00:34:01,700 --> 00:34:03,500
So yeah, thanks a lot.

747
00:34:03,500 --> 00:34:10,989

748
00:34:10,989 --> 00:34:13,600
So I have a question.

749
00:34:13,600 --> 00:34:16,270
I don't know anything
about SDM, but it

750
00:34:16,270 --> 00:34:20,540
seems, as understood, it's
very good for long-term memory.

751
00:34:20,540 --> 00:34:25,639
And I have a question--
what's your hypothesis of what

752
00:34:25,639 --> 00:34:29,610
we should be doing
for short-term memory.

753
00:34:29,610 --> 00:34:31,540
Because it seems that--

754
00:34:31,540 --> 00:34:35,130
so if you have this
