have to separate out

1003
00:45:28,010 --> 00:45:29,390
the structure and
the experiences

1004
00:45:29,390 --> 00:45:31,765
you're having, so that you
can reuse the structural part,

1005
00:45:31,765 --> 00:45:34,920
which appears very
often in the world.

1006
00:45:34,920 --> 00:45:37,080
And so separating
memories from structure.

1007
00:45:37,080 --> 00:45:40,650
And so, you know, here's
our separation of the two.

1008
00:45:40,650 --> 00:45:44,640
We have our dude wandering
around this like 2D grid world.

1009
00:45:44,640 --> 00:45:48,150
And you want to separate out
the fact that there's 2D space,

1010
00:45:48,150 --> 00:45:50,973
and it's 2D space that has
these rules underlying it.

1011
00:45:50,973 --> 00:45:53,640
And in a particular instance, in
the environment that you're in,

1012
00:45:53,640 --> 00:45:56,220
you need to be able to recall
which objects are at which

1013
00:45:56,220 --> 00:45:58,137
locations in the environment.

1014
00:45:58,137 --> 00:45:59,970
So in this case, it's
like oh, this position

1015
00:45:59,970 --> 00:46:01,740
has an orange house,
this position--

1016
00:46:01,740 --> 00:46:03,983
that's green, sorry,
orange, red, and pink.

1017
00:46:03,983 --> 00:46:05,400
And so you have
to bind those two,

1018
00:46:05,400 --> 00:46:07,140
you have to be like, whenever
you realize that you're back

1019
00:46:07,140 --> 00:46:09,930
in this position, recall that
is the observation you're

1020
00:46:09,930 --> 00:46:11,940
going to see there.

1021
00:46:11,940 --> 00:46:14,760
And so this model that
we're going to build

1022
00:46:14,760 --> 00:46:18,090
is some model that
tries to achieve this.

1023
00:46:18,090 --> 00:46:19,590
New task-- and so
when you enter it,

1024
00:46:19,590 --> 00:46:20,670
imagine you enter
a new environment

1025
00:46:20,670 --> 00:46:23,070
with the same structure, you
wander around and realize

1026
00:46:23,070 --> 00:46:23,730
it's the same structure.

1027
00:46:23,730 --> 00:46:25,230
All you have to do is
bind the new things

1028
00:46:25,230 --> 00:46:27,390
that you see to the locations,
and then you're done.

1029
00:46:27,390 --> 00:46:27,890
Task done.

1030
00:46:27,890 --> 00:46:30,790
You know how the world works.

1031
00:46:30,790 --> 00:46:33,730
So this is what neuroscientists
mean by a cognitive map,

1032
00:46:33,730 --> 00:46:36,010
is this idea of separating
out and understanding

1033
00:46:36,010 --> 00:46:38,170
the structure that you can
reuse in new situations.

1034
00:46:38,170 --> 00:46:42,538
And yeah, this model that
was built in the lab,

1035
00:46:42,538 --> 00:46:44,080
is a model of this
process happening,

1036
00:46:44,080 --> 00:46:45,340
of this separation
between the two of them

1037
00:46:45,340 --> 00:46:47,350
and how you use them
to do new inferences.

1038
00:46:47,350 --> 00:46:49,457
And this is the
bit that's supposed

1039
00:46:49,457 --> 00:46:50,540
looked like a transformer.

1040
00:46:50,540 --> 00:46:51,915
So that's a general
introduction,

1041
00:46:51,915 --> 00:46:54,060
and then we'll dive into
it a little more now.

1042
00:46:54,060 --> 00:46:56,220
Makes sense, though,
the broad picture?

1043
00:46:56,220 --> 00:46:59,370
Good, silence I'll
assume is good.

1044
00:46:59,370 --> 00:47:02,910
So we'll start off
with some brain stuff.

1045
00:47:02,910 --> 00:47:05,220
So there's a long
stream of evidence

1046
00:47:05,220 --> 00:47:07,170
from spatial navigation
that the brain

1047
00:47:07,170 --> 00:47:08,830
is doing something like this.

1048
00:47:08,830 --> 00:47:11,550
I mean, I think you can probably
imagine how you, yourself, are

1049
00:47:11,550 --> 00:47:14,352
doing this already when
you go to a new city,

1050
00:47:14,352 --> 00:47:15,810
or you're like
trying to understand

1051
00:47:15,810 --> 00:47:17,310
a new task that has some
structure that you recognize

1052
00:47:17,310 --> 00:47:17,977
from previously.

1053
00:47:17,977 --> 00:47:19,410
You can see how
this is something

1054
00:47:19,410 --> 00:47:20,670
that you're probably doing.

1055
00:47:20,670 --> 00:47:22,712
But spatial navigation is
an area in neuroscience

1056
00:47:22,712 --> 00:47:25,350
which had like a huge stream
of discoveries over the last 50

1057
00:47:25,350 --> 00:47:26,430
years.

1058
00:47:26,430 --> 00:47:28,740
And a lot of evidence
of the neural basis

1059
00:47:28,740 --> 00:47:29,580
of this computation.

1060
00:47:29,580 --> 00:47:31,830
So we're going to talk through
some of those examples.

1061
00:47:31,830 --> 00:47:34,680
The earliest of these,
psychologists like Tolman,

1062
00:47:34,680 --> 00:47:37,560
who were showing that
rats, in this case,

1063
00:47:37,560 --> 00:47:39,667
can do this kind of path
integration of structure.

1064
00:47:39,667 --> 00:47:42,000
So the way this work is they
got put at a start position

1065
00:47:42,000 --> 00:47:44,610
here, down at the bottom
S, and they got trained

1066
00:47:44,610 --> 00:47:47,050
that this route up
here got your reward.

1067
00:47:47,050 --> 00:47:49,080
So this is the maze
they had to run around.

1068
00:47:49,080 --> 00:47:50,220
Then they were asked--

1069
00:47:50,220 --> 00:47:52,110
they were put in this new--

1070
00:47:52,110 --> 00:47:53,740
the same thing, but
they blocked off

1071
00:47:53,740 --> 00:47:55,657
this path that takes
this long, winding route,

1072
00:47:55,657 --> 00:47:58,210
and given instead a selection
of all these arms to go down.

1073
00:47:58,210 --> 00:48:00,610
And they look at which
path the rat goes down.

1074
00:48:00,610 --> 00:48:02,500
And the finding is
that the rat goes down

1075
00:48:02,500 --> 00:48:05,090
the one that corresponds to
heading off in this direction.

1076
00:48:05,090 --> 00:48:07,600
So the rat has somehow
not just learned--

1077
00:48:07,600 --> 00:48:10,405
one option of this is like
blind memorization of actions

1078
00:48:10,405 --> 00:48:12,280
that I need to take in
order to route around.

1079
00:48:12,280 --> 00:48:13,780
Instead, no, it's
learning actually

1080
00:48:13,780 --> 00:48:16,367
that embedding the reward and
its understanding of 2D space,

1081
00:48:16,367 --> 00:48:18,700
and taking the direct route
there even though it's never

1082
00:48:18,700 --> 00:48:19,510
taken it before.

1083
00:48:19,510 --> 00:48:22,150
So there's evidence that rats
are doing this as well as us.

1084
00:48:22,150 --> 00:48:24,670
And then, a series
of neural discoveries

1085
00:48:24,670 --> 00:48:28,680
about the basis of this,
so John O'Keefe stuck

1086
00:48:28,680 --> 00:48:31,180
an electrode in the hippocampus,
which is a brain area we'll

1087
00:48:31,180 --> 00:48:33,830
talk more about, and found
these things called place cells.

1088
00:48:33,830 --> 00:48:35,650
So what I'm plotting
here is each

1089
00:48:35,650 --> 00:48:38,500
of these columns
is a single neuron,

1090
00:48:38,500 --> 00:48:41,195
and the mouse or rat,
I can't remember,

1091
00:48:41,195 --> 00:48:42,820
is running around a
square environment.

1092
00:48:42,820 --> 00:48:47,380
The black lines are the path the
rodent traces out through time,

1093
00:48:47,380 --> 00:48:49,510
and you put a red dot
down every time you see

1094
00:48:49,510 --> 00:48:50,895
this individual neuron spike.

1095
00:48:50,895 --> 00:48:52,270
And then, the
bottom plot of this

1096
00:48:52,270 --> 00:48:54,340
is just a smooth
version of that spike.

1097
00:48:54,340 --> 00:48:55,760
So that firing rate.

1098
00:48:55,760 --> 00:48:57,370
Which you can think of as
like the activity of a neuron

1099
00:48:57,370 --> 00:48:58,030
and neural network.

1100
00:48:58,030 --> 00:48:59,920
That's the analogy that
people usually draw.

1101
00:48:59,920 --> 00:49:01,420
And so these ones are
called place cells,

1102
00:49:01,420 --> 00:49:02,500
because they're
neurons that respond

1103
00:49:02,500 --> 00:49:04,210
in a particular
position in space.

1104
00:49:04,210 --> 00:49:06,097
And in the '70s, this
was huge excitement.

1105
00:49:06,097 --> 00:49:08,680
People had been studying mainly
like sensory systems and motor

1106
00:49:08,680 --> 00:49:09,260
output.

1107
00:49:09,260 --> 00:49:11,008
And suddenly, a deep
cognitive variable

1108
00:49:11,008 --> 00:49:13,300
place, something you never--
you don't have a signaler,

1109
00:49:13,300 --> 00:49:15,340
but somehow there's
this signal for what

1110
00:49:15,340 --> 00:49:19,890
looks like position in the brain
in very understandable ways.

1111
00:49:19,890 --> 00:49:21,420
The next step in--

1112
00:49:21,420 --> 00:49:23,670
the biggest step, I guess,
in this chain of discovery,

1113
00:49:23,670 --> 00:49:26,970
is the Moser lab which
is a group in Norway,

1114
00:49:26,970 --> 00:49:29,372
they stuck an electron in a
different area of the brain,

1115
00:49:29,372 --> 00:49:30,580
the medial entorhinal cortex.

1116
00:49:30,580 --> 00:49:32,300
And so this is the
hippocampal entorhinal system

1117
00:49:32,300 --> 00:49:33,060
we're going to be talking about.

1118
00:49:33,060 --> 00:49:34,985
And they found this
neuron called a grid cell.

1119
00:49:34,985 --> 00:49:36,360
So again, the same
plot structure

1120
00:49:36,360 --> 00:49:38,340
that I'm showing here,
but instead, these neurons

1121
00:49:38,340 --> 00:49:39,923
respond not in one
position in a room,

1122
00:49:39,923 --> 00:49:44,100
but at a hexagonal lattice
of positions in a room.

1123
00:49:44,100 --> 00:49:48,060
So these two, I guess I'm
showing to you, because they

1124
00:49:48,060 --> 00:49:51,510
really motivate the underlying
neural basis of this kind

1125
00:49:51,510 --> 00:49:54,360
of spatial cognition,
embodying the structure

1126
00:49:54,360 --> 00:49:56,195
of this space in some way.

1127
00:49:56,195 --> 00:49:57,570
That's a very
surprising finding,

1128
00:49:57,570 --> 00:49:59,433
why are neurons choosing
to represent things

1129
00:49:59,433 --> 00:50:00,600
with this hexagonal lattice?

1130
00:50:00,600 --> 00:50:03,420
It's like yeah, provoked
a lot of research since.

1131
00:50:03,420 --> 00:50:06,610
And broadly, there's been many
more discoveries in this area.

1132
00:50:06,610 --> 00:50:08,820
So there's place cells
I've talked to you

1133
00:50:08,820 --> 00:50:10,980
about, grid cells,
cells that respond based

1134
00:50:10,980 --> 00:50:14,190
on the location of not yourself
but another animal, cells that

1135
00:50:14,190 --> 00:50:16,980
respond when your head is facing
a particular direction, cells

1136
00:50:16,980 --> 00:50:19,600
that respond to when you're
a particular distance away

1137
00:50:19,600 --> 00:50:20,630
from an object.

1138
00:50:20,630 --> 00:50:24,820
So like I'm one step south of
an object, that kind of cell.

1139
00:50:24,820 --> 00:50:26,590
Cells that respond
to a reward position.

1140
00:50:26,590 --> 00:50:28,392
Cells respond to
vectors, to boundaries,

1141
00:50:28,392 --> 00:50:30,100
cells respond to
[INAUDIBLE],, so there's

1142
00:50:30,100 --> 00:50:31,960
like all sorts, all
kinds of structure

1143
00:50:31,960 --> 00:50:35,290
that this pair of
brain structures,

1144
00:50:35,290 --> 00:50:37,330
the hippocampus
here, this red area,

1145
00:50:37,330 --> 00:50:40,570
and the entorhinal cortex
this blue area here, which

1146
00:50:40,570 --> 00:50:44,993
is conserved across-- a lot
of species are represented.

1147
00:50:44,993 --> 00:50:47,160
There's also finally one
finding in this that's fun,

1148
00:50:47,160 --> 00:50:51,370
is they did an fMRI experiment
on London taxi cab drivers.

1149
00:50:51,370 --> 00:50:53,220
And I don't know
if you know this,

1150
00:50:53,220 --> 00:50:54,720
but the London
taxicab drivers, they

1151
00:50:54,720 --> 00:50:56,830
do a thing called
the knowledge, which

1152
00:50:56,830 --> 00:50:58,830
is a two-year long test
where they have to learn

1153
00:50:58,830 --> 00:51:00,240
every street in London.

1154
00:51:00,240 --> 00:51:02,730
And the idea is, the
tests go something like,

1155
00:51:02,730 --> 00:51:04,980
oh, there's a traffic jam
here, and a roadwork here,

1156
00:51:04,980 --> 00:51:08,482
and I need to get from like
Camden Town down to Wandsworth

1157
00:51:08,482 --> 00:51:09,690
in the quickest way possible.

1158
00:51:09,690 --> 00:51:10,660
What route would you go?

1159
00:51:10,660 --> 00:51:11,490
And they have to
tell you which route

1160
00:51:11,490 --> 00:51:12,930
they're going be able to
take through all the roads,

1161
00:51:12,930 --> 00:51:14,760
and how they would
re-plan if they found

1162
00:51:14,760 --> 00:51:16,030
a stop-- those kind of things.

1163
00:51:16,030 --> 00:51:17,580
So it's like
intense, you see them

1164
00:51:17,580 --> 00:51:20,250
like driving around sometimes
learning all of these routes

1165
00:51:20,250 --> 00:51:21,090
with little maps.

1166
00:51:21,090 --> 00:51:24,090
They're being made a little
bit obsolete by Google Maps,

1167
00:51:24,090 --> 00:51:26,190
but you know, luckily
they got them before--

1168
00:51:26,190 --> 00:51:28,150
this experiment was done
before that was true.

1169
00:51:28,150 --> 00:51:30,300
And so they've got
here is a measure

1170
00:51:30,300 --> 00:51:32,010
of the size of your
hippocampus using

1171
00:51:32,010 --> 00:51:34,110
fMRI, versus how long
you've been a taxi cab

1172
00:51:34,110 --> 00:51:34,950
driver in months.

1173
00:51:34,950 --> 00:51:36,750
And the claim is basically
the longer you're a taxi cab

1174
00:51:36,750 --> 00:51:37,950
driver, the bigger
your hippocampus,

1175
00:51:37,950 --> 00:51:39,450
because the more
you're having to do

1176
00:51:39,450 --> 00:51:40,850
this kind of spatial reasoning.

1177
00:51:40,850 --> 00:51:44,110
So that's a big set of
evidence that these brain areas

1178
00:51:44,110 --> 00:51:46,220
are doing something
to do with space.

1179
00:51:46,220 --> 00:51:49,030
But there's a lot of evidence
that there's something more

1180
00:51:49,030 --> 00:51:51,575
than that, something non-spatial
going on in these areas.

1181
00:51:51,575 --> 00:51:53,200
And we're going to
build these together

1182
00:51:53,200 --> 00:51:56,870
to make the broader claim about
this underlying structural

1183
00:51:56,870 --> 00:51:57,370
inference.

1184
00:51:57,370 --> 00:52:00,800
And so I'm going to talk
through a couple of those.

1185
00:52:00,800 --> 00:52:03,700
The first one of these is
a guy called patient HM--

1186
00:52:03,700 --> 00:52:07,300
this is the most studied
patient in medical history.

1187
00:52:07,300 --> 00:52:11,898
He had epilepsy, and to
cure intractable epilepsy,

1188
00:52:11,898 --> 00:52:13,690
you have to cut out
the brain region that's

1189
00:52:13,690 --> 00:52:16,970
causing these seizure-like
events in your brain.

1190
00:52:16,970 --> 00:52:19,270
And in this case, the
epilepsy was coming

1191
00:52:19,270 --> 00:52:20,600
from the guy's hippocampus.

1192
00:52:20,600 --> 00:52:22,760
So they bilaterally
lesioned his hippocampus--

1193
00:52:22,760 --> 00:52:25,180
they cut out both
of his hippocampi.

1194
00:52:25,180 --> 00:52:28,060
And it turned out that this
guy then had terrible amnesia.

1195
00:52:28,060 --> 00:52:29,620
He never formed
another memory again.

1196
00:52:29,620 --> 00:52:31,703
And he could only recall
memories from a long time

1197
00:52:31,703 --> 00:52:34,390
before the surgery happened.

1198
00:52:34,390 --> 00:52:38,260
But yeah, so experiments
showed a lot of this stuff

1199
00:52:38,260 --> 00:52:41,220
about how we understand
the neural basis of memory.

1200
00:52:41,220 --> 00:52:43,477
Things like he could
learn to do motor tasks--

1201
00:52:43,477 --> 00:52:45,560
so somehow the motor tasks
are being [INAUDIBLE]..

1202
00:52:45,560 --> 00:52:47,420
For example, they gave him
some very difficult motor

1203
00:52:47,420 --> 00:52:49,580
coordination tasks that people
can't generally do, but can

1204
00:52:49,580 --> 00:52:51,788
with a lot of practice, and
he got very good at this.

1205
00:52:51,788 --> 00:52:53,840
Eventually, and was as
good as other people

1206
00:52:53,840 --> 00:52:56,000
at learning to do that, he had
no recollection of ever doing

1207
00:52:56,000 --> 00:52:56,490
the task.

1208
00:52:56,490 --> 00:52:57,800
So he'd go into the
do this new task,

1209
00:52:57,800 --> 00:52:59,310
and be like, I've
never seen this before.

1210
00:52:59,310 --> 00:53:00,320
I have no idea what
you're asking me to do.

1211
00:53:00,320 --> 00:53:01,610
And you do it amazingly.

1212
00:53:01,610 --> 00:53:02,480
And be like-- yeah.

1213
00:53:02,480 --> 00:53:05,185
So there's some evidence
there that the hippocampus

1214
00:53:05,185 --> 00:53:07,310
is involved in at least
some parts of memory, which

1215
00:53:07,310 --> 00:53:09,227
seems a bit separate to
this stuff about space

1216
00:53:09,227 --> 00:53:10,910
that I've been
talking to you about.

1217
00:53:10,910 --> 00:53:13,320
The second of these
is imagining things.

1218
00:53:13,320 --> 00:53:15,680
So this is actually a
paper by Demis Hassabis,

1219
00:53:15,680 --> 00:53:19,280
who, before he was DeepMind
head, was a neuroscientist.

1220
00:53:19,280 --> 00:53:21,980
And here, maybe you
can't read that, I'll

1221
00:53:21,980 --> 00:53:23,180
read some of these out.

1222
00:53:23,180 --> 00:53:25,910
You're asked to imagine you're
lying on a white sandy beach

1223
00:53:25,910 --> 00:53:27,110
in a beautiful tropical bay.

1224
00:53:27,110 --> 00:53:29,000
And so the control, this
bottom one says things like,

1225
00:53:29,000 --> 00:53:31,042
it's very hot, and the
sun is beating down on me,

1226
00:53:31,042 --> 00:53:33,050
the sand underneath me
is almost unbearably hot.

1227
00:53:33,050 --> 00:53:35,240
I can hear the sounds of small
wavelets lapping on the beach.

1228
00:53:35,240 --> 00:53:36,920
The sea is gorgeous,
aquamarine color.

1229
00:53:36,920 --> 00:53:38,900
You know, like so a
nice, lucid description

1230
00:53:38,900 --> 00:53:40,940
of this beachy scene.

1231
00:53:40,940 --> 00:53:44,660
Whereas the person with a
hippocampal damage says,

1232
00:53:44,660 --> 00:53:46,970
as for seeing, I can't really,
apart from just the sky.

1233
00:53:46,970 --> 00:53:50,090
I can hear the sound of
seagulls, and of the sea.

1234
00:53:50,090 --> 00:53:53,480
I could feel the grain of
sand beneath my fingers.

1235
00:53:53,480 --> 00:53:56,000
And then, like yeah,
struggles, basically.

1236
00:53:56,000 --> 00:53:58,320
Really struggles to do
this, imagine this scenario.

1237
00:53:58,320 --> 00:54:00,390
Some of the things really
are very surprising.

1238
00:54:00,390 --> 00:54:06,060
So the last of these is this
transitive inference task.

1239
00:54:06,060 --> 00:54:07,670
So transitive
inference-- A is greater

1240
00:54:07,670 --> 00:54:11,360
